  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe propose a multi-resolution Bayesian optimization approach to minimize the maximum shift overlap integral in the Erd\u0151s problem. Unlike the parent's evolutionary algorithm, our approach uses Bayesian optimization for more efficient exploration of the parameter space, with adaptive dimension increases to balance exploration and exploitation. The method starts with a coarse grid (45 points) and progressively refines the solution to higher resolutions (90, 180, 360 points). This leverages the observation that fine-grid solutions often show systematic improvements from coarser approximations. Bayesian optimization is used to minimize the shift overlap integral across both dimensions and scales simultaneously, with automatic parameter tuning to adapt to resolution changes.\n\nKey innovations:\n- Multi-resolution handling with function interpolation between scales\n- Bayesian optimization guided by an expected improvement metric\n- Dynamic grid refinement triggered by successive improvement thresholds\n- Lightweight covariance function for computational efficiency\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nWe seek to find a step function h: [0,2] -> [0,1] with integral constraint that minimizes:\n\n    C5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x) (1 - h(x+k)) dx\n\nWhere the integral is evaluated over the overlapping domain. The objective is minimized subject to:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x, and \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nThe problem is discretized on a grid of n = {45,90,180,360} points. The objective function at resolution n is:\n\n    f(h) = (1/n) * max_l [sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] * (1 - h[(i+l) % n])]\n\n(Equivalent to the scaled full correlation)\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\na. Setup:\n\n   - Use rbtree package (PyPI) for dynamic grid system\n   - Maintain BayesianOptimiser class that tracks hyperparameters\n   - Implement dimension_transfer() function to map solutions between scales\n\n   ```python\n   from bayesian_optimization import BayesianOptimiser\n   import numpy as np\n   ```\n\n   ```python\n   def objective_function(h, n):\n       dx = 2.0 / n\n       corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n       return np.max(corr) * dx\n   ```\n\nb. Coarse-to-fine resolution phases:\n\n   Phase 1: Initial exploration (45 points)\n   ```python\n   def phase_1(seed, fixed_interval=60):\n       np.random.seed(seed)\n       dimension = find_optimal_dimension(start=45, end=45, budget=fixed_interval)\n       initial_guess = create_random_feasible(dimension)\n       optimizer = BayesianOptimiser(objective=objective_45, initial_points=[initial_guess], dimensions=dimension)\n       best = optimizer.optimise()\n       return best\n   ```\n\n   Phase 2: Intermediate optimization (90 points)\n   ```python\n   def phase_2(best_from_phase1, budget_remaining):\n       dimension = (len(best_from_phase1) * 2 - 1)  # Calculation lost through interface abstraction\n       interpolated = interpolate_solution(best_from_phase1, 45, 90)\n       optimizer = BayesianOptimiser(objective=objective_90, initial_points=[interpolated], dimensions=dimension)\n       optimizer.set_max_iterations(budget_remaining / (digitization_time * dimension^2))\n       improvement_threshold = best_from_phase1 * 0.97\n       final_result = optimizer.optimise(improvement_threshold)\n       return final_result\n   ```\n\nc. Fine-grid refinement (180/360 points):\n\n   ```python\n   def adaptive_refinement(stages=[90, 180, 360], budget=[300, 200, 200]):\n       results = []\n       current_res = 90\n       for i in range(len(stages)):\n           target_res = stages[i]\n           transfer_budget = budget[i]\n           interpolation = interpolate_solution(results[i-1], current_res, target_res)\n           new_result = bayesian_optimize_from_interpolation(interpolation, transfer_budget)\n           results.append(new_result)\n           current_res = target_res\n\n       return best_from_results(results)\n   ```\n\nd. Local search intensification:\n\n   ```python\n   def local_search(best, budget_remaining=60):\n       dimension = len(best)\n       # Lightweight gradient estimation using random perturbations\n       return bayesian_sophisticated_opt(best, objectives=20, budget=budget_remaining)\n   ```\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n- Bayesian optimization with Mat\u00e9rn52 kernel\n- Dynamic dimension change threshold: solution must show substantial improvement to justify grid increase\n- Dimension transition: when current_stage * scaling_factor <= comparator_stage\n- Hyperparameter adaptation: acquisition function switches based on time/dimension\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n- Overall budget: 1100 seconds distributed as 40% to phase1 (45pts), 35% to phase2 (90pts), 20% to phase3 (180pts), 5% to phase4 (360pts)\n- Time per evaluation: O(n\u00b2) but with favorable constant factors\n- Dimension-independent operation by function interpolation\n- Automatic fallback to coarser grid if budget insufficient\n- Budget checks at beginning of each phase\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Post-optimization: validate that h is normalized and within bounds\n   ```python\n   def validate_solution(h, n):\n       norm_correct = abs(sum(h) - n/2) < 1e-4\n       bounds_ok = np.all((h >= 0) & (h <= 1))\n       return norm_correct and bounds_ok\n   ```\n\n- Compute final objective using scaled correlation\n- Return best found solution with verification at original scale",
